From tru64-unix-managers  Wed Oct 25 18:48:30 2000
From: "Mark S. Yamnicky" <marco () nirvana ! es ! hac ! com>
Date: Wed, 25 Oct 2000 18:48:30 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: motif libraries
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=97249976727728

> I recently installed an application called Mathematica.
> When running certain operations I get a loader failure:
> "cannot map libpsres.so". Mathematica tech supports states
> it's a result of a Mathematica kernel utility that cannot
> be statically linked to the Motif libraries. As it turns
> out, True64 Motif does not have this library installed.

Many thanks to:
Nikola Milutinovic
Peter Stern
Oisin McGuinness

=46rom Oisin
On 4.0D libpsres.so is installed in /usr/shlib as part of OSFX11425, =
"Basic X=20
environment". A quick peek suggests strongly that it is part of the DPS =
"Display=20
Post Script" system, something to manipulate PostScript resources. (?)
Since Adobe announced a couple of years ago they weren't supporting DPS =
anymore,
the major Unix vendors including Compaq had to retire it. Thus 5.x =
doesn't=20
include this stuff anymore.  Copying the shared library from a 4.0D =
system may=20
help you a little, unless the application actually tries to use it, in =
which=20
case you may have trouble=A0 unless your X supports DPS....


=46rom Peter...
This lib (along with libpsres.a, libdps.{a,so} and libdpstk.{a,so}) are
no longer in the OSFX11xxx and OSFXLIBAxxx subsets (?).  To solve this
problem (and some others), I simply copied those libraries from a=20
Tru64 V4.0E system to my V4.0F system and everything seems to work.

=46rom Nix...
It could be one of the old Motif libraries. You could try installing
Motif 1.1 support. It is one of the options on initial installation.
Just put your T64 installation CD into the drive, mount it and go to
./ALPHA/... dir and run "setld".

