Thank you to :
lhercaud@bouyguestelecom.fr
Jonas.Furrer@BIT.admin.ch
tpb@doctor.zk3.dec.com
alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com

for their answers

        In the context error 5 is usually an I/O error.  You
        may have one or bad blocks that have corrupted the
        particular file system.  Reading the block triggers
        a domain or system panic.  Check the error log for
        I/O errors.  AdvFS may be logging more extensive
        information about the errors in one of the log files
        under /var/adm.  This may provide detail about which
        files are affected or whether the problem is in the
        file system metadata.  You may have to replace/repair
        the disk, recreate the file system and then restore
        from a good backup.

        Without more information about the underlying I/O
        configuration it is hard to provide good advice.
-----------------------------

I've replaced the disk and other problems occur. Just to let you know ,
it was a problem with one of the 2 memory cards where the slots 5 and 6
where faulty ....

Daniel

